&lt;/table&gt;
&lt;p style="margin-top: 0pt; margin-left: 30.25pt; margin-bottom: 9pt;"&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p style="margin-top: 0pt; margin-left: 21.6pt; margin-bottom: 9pt; text-indent: -21.6pt; page-break-after: avoid; -aw-import: list-item; -aw-list-level-number: 0; -aw-list-number-format: '%0.'; -aw-list-number-styles: 'decimal'; -aw-list-number-values: '4'; -aw-list-padding-sml: 13.09pt;"&gt;&lt;font style="-aw-import: ignore;"&gt;&lt;font style="font-weight: bold; text-transform: uppercase;"&gt;4.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;font style="width: 13.09pt; font: 7pt 'Times New Roman'; display: inline-block; -aw-import: spaces;"&gt;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160; &lt;/font&gt;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;font style="font-weight: bold; text-transform: uppercase;"&gt;LONG&lt;/font&gt;&lt;font style="font-weight: bold; text-transform: uppercase;"&gt;&amp;#8209;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;font style="font-weight: bold; text-transform: uppercase;"&gt;TERM DEBT AND OTHER OBLIGATIONS&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p style="margin-top: 0pt; margin-left: 21.6pt; margin-bottom: 9pt;"&gt;&lt;font style="font-weight: bold;"&gt;Obligations Restructuring Agreement&lt;/font&gt;&amp;#8212;Effective October&amp;#160;16, 1996, the Cooperative entered into an Obligations Restructuring Agreement (&amp;#8220;ORA&amp;#8221; and subsequent amendments described below) with its long&amp;#8209;term lenders. Under the terms of the ORA, the National Rural Utilities Cooperative Finance Corporation (CFC) purchased the outstanding notes issued by the Cooperative to the United States Rural Utilities Service (RUS). CFC then agreed to modify the payment terms of the Cooperative&amp;#8217;s outstanding mortgage&amp;#8209;backed obligations owed to CFC, including the payment terms under the RUS indebtedness that had been acquired by CFC. Effective December&amp;#160;4, 1998, the Cooperative purchased the Bonanza Plant and other assets in a Recapitalization and Settlement Agreement (the &amp;#8220;1998 Recapitalization Agreement&amp;#8221;). As a result of the 1998&amp;#160;Recapitalization Agreement, the Cooperative issued new promissory notes to CFC and General Electric Credit Corporation (GECC); in exchange, the creditors of the Cooperative agreed to waive all claims under pre&amp;#8209;existing indebtedness and to forgive all outstanding amounts due from the Cooperative upon the payment of the replacement notes.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p style="margin-top: 0pt; margin-left: 21.6pt; margin-bottom: 9pt;"&gt;As a result of the ORA and the 1998&amp;#160;Recapitalization Agreement, the Cooperative&amp;#8217;s future payment obligations under its pre&amp;#8209;existing debt arrangements were substantially reduced. The renegotiated payment terms under the replacement notes provided for stipulated quarterly minimum payments, and Additional Payments contingent upon net cash flows from operations as set forth in the terms of the replacement notes.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p style="margin-top: 0pt; margin-left: 21.6pt; margin-bottom: 9pt;"&gt;In a series of amendments executed among the parties to the ORA, the interest of GECC in and to long&amp;#8209;term indebtedness of the Cooperative was purchased by CFC and further modified according to the terms of these various amendments. Effective January&amp;#160;1, 2018, the Cooperative entered into a Conformed Obligations Restructuring Agreement (&amp;#8220;CORA&amp;#8221;) with its remaining long&amp;#8209;term lender which consolidated, modified, superseded and restated the effective terms of the original ORA, and the subsequent amendments thereto.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p style="margin-top: 0pt; margin-left: 21.6pt; margin-bottom: 9pt;"&gt;Under the terms of the CORA, CFC agreed, among other things: (i)&amp;#160;to modify the future scheduled quarterly minimum payments through the remaining term of the CORA; and (ii)&amp;#160;to accept a separate, additional new promissory note in the amount of $10,000,000 (the 2026 Note) with payment terms that are not subject to the provisions of the CORA.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p style="margin-top: 0pt; margin-left: 21.6pt; margin-bottom: 9pt;"&gt;&lt;font style="font-weight: bold;"&gt;Prepayments from Available Extra Cash Flow&lt;/font&gt;&amp;#8212; Under the terms of the CORA, the Cooperative agreed to make payment equal to 100% of available extra net cash flow, if any, net of certain expenditures, debt service payments, additions to permitted reserves, and other specified adjustments, that may be generated each year through the term of the CORA (&amp;#8220;Available Extra Cash Flow&amp;#8221;). The calculated Available Extra Cash Flow for the year ended December&amp;#160;31, 2025, was approximately $2,887,000. The calculated Available Extra Cash Flow for the year ended December&amp;#160;31, 2024, was approximately $16,272,000, which was applied to reduce future scheduled minimum payments. The Available Extra Cash Flow is due no later than 90&amp;#160;days after the end of each calendar year.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p style="margin-top: 0pt; margin-left: 21.6pt; margin-bottom: 9pt;"&gt;&lt;font style="font-weight: bold;"&gt;Available Payment Credits&lt;/font&gt;&amp;#8212; Under the terms of the CORA, If the Cooperative cannot make a stipulated quarterly minimum payment from the current year net cash flow, the Cooperative has to apply certain amounts previously paid under the terms of the ORA as a credit (&amp;#8220;Available Payment Credits&amp;#8221;) towards the unpaid amounts of quarterly minimum payments as those quarterly installments fall due. Application of such Available Payment Credits in accordance with the terms of the CORA will not result in any event of default to the Cooperative. As of December&amp;#160;31, 2024 the balance of Available Payment Credits otherwise available for use as future credits totaled approximately $61,450,000. During 2025, according to the terms of the CORA, the Cooperative applied approximately $32,273,000 of Available Payment Credits towards its 2025 quarterly installments. The remaining balance of unused credits are no longer available for use as of January 1, 2026.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p style="margin-top: 0pt; margin-left: 21.6pt; margin-bottom: 9pt;"&gt;&lt;font style="font-weight: bold;"&gt;Interest Rates&lt;/font&gt;&amp;#8212;The Cooperative has re&amp;#8209;evaluated forecasted cash flows, including consideration of minimum payments, prepayments, and Available Payment Credits through the term of the CORA, and compared the forecasted cash flows to the stipulated minimum payments. Based on a present value analysis from modeled future payments, the imputed interest rates of the notes have changed based on the remaining unpaid amount of future quarterly minimum payments and future estimated cash flows.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p style="margin-top: 0pt; margin-left: 21.6pt; margin-bottom: 9pt;"&gt;&lt;font style="font-weight: bold;"&gt;Covenants&lt;/font&gt;&amp;#8212; The CORA contains debt covenants which address investments that may be held, and sets forth requirements with respect to budgeted capital expenditures and various reports required to be submitted periodically to CFC. Under the terms of the CORA, the Cooperative agreed to certain limitations on the application of Available Payment Credits as described above. The Cooperative is of the opinion that the Cooperative is in compliance with all of the debt covenants as of December&amp;#160;31, 2025.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p style="margin-top: 0pt; margin-left: 21.6pt; margin-bottom: 9pt; page-break-after: avoid;"&gt;&lt;font style="font-weight: bold;"&gt;Notes Payable&lt;/font&gt;&amp;#8212;Notes payable as of December&amp;#160;31, 2025 and 2024, were as follows:&lt;/p&gt;
