&lt;p style="margin-top: 0pt; margin-left: 30.25pt; margin-bottom: 9pt;"&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p style="margin-top: 0pt; margin-left: 21.6pt; margin-bottom: 9pt;"&gt;In accordance with the provisions of ASC&amp;#160;740, &lt;font style="font-style: italic;"&gt;Income Taxes&lt;/font&gt;, the Cooperative records a liability for unrecognized tax benefits. The Cooperative&amp;#8217;s unrecognized tax benefits are tax positions for which the ultimate deductibility is highly certain but for which there is uncertainty about the timing of such deductibility. The Cooperative&amp;#8217;s policy is to recognize accrued interest and penalties related to uncertain tax positions in income tax expense. As of December&amp;#160;31, 2025 and 2024, the Cooperative had no liability or related accrued interest and penalties related to uncertain tax positions. The Cooperative does not anticipate that unrecognized tax benefits will significantly increase or decrease within 12&amp;#160;months of the reporting date. As of December&amp;#160;31, 2025, with limited exceptions, the Cooperative is no longer subject to examination by taxing authorities for tax years prior to 2021 for federal and prior to 2020 for state purposes.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p style="margin-top: 0pt; margin-left: 21.6pt; margin-bottom: 9pt; text-indent: -21.6pt; page-break-after: avoid; -aw-import: list-item; -aw-list-level-number: 0; -aw-list-number-format: '%0.'; -aw-list-number-styles: 'decimal'; -aw-list-number-values: '6'; -aw-list-padding-sml: 13.09pt;"&gt;&lt;font style="-aw-import: ignore;"&gt;&lt;font style="font-weight: bold; text-transform: uppercase;"&gt;6.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;font style="width: 13.09pt; font: 7pt 'Times New Roman'; display: inline-block; -aw-import: spaces;"&gt;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160; &lt;/font&gt;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;font style="font-weight: bold; text-transform: uppercase;"&gt;EMPLOYEE BENEFIT PLANS&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p style="margin-top: 0pt; margin-left: 21.6pt; margin-bottom: 9pt;"&gt;All employees of the Cooperative with one&amp;#160;year of service who have attained age&amp;#160;21 are provided pension benefits through participation in the National Rural Electric Cooperative Association&amp;#8217;s (NRECA) Retirement and Security Defined Benefit Pension Plan (the &amp;#8220;Plan&amp;#8221;). The Cooperative contributed either directly or as a contribution prepayment to NRECA approximately $5,273,000 and $5,439,000 for the Plan and the Pension Restoration Plan described below for the years ended December&amp;#160;31, 2025 and 2024, respectively. The policy of the Cooperative is to fund the pension costs accrued. The NRECA Retirement and Security Program is a multi&amp;#8209;employer plan. In the Plan, a &amp;#8220;zone status&amp;#8221; determination is not required, and therefore not determined, under the Pension Protection Act of 2006 (PPA). In addition, the accumulated benefit obligations and the Plan assets are not determined or allocated separately by individual employer. In total, the Plan was over 80% funded on both January&amp;#160;1, 2025 and January&amp;#160;1, 2024, based on the PPA funding target and PPA actuarial value of assets on those dates. Because the provisions of the PPA do not apply to the Plan, funding improvement plans and surcharges are not applicable. Future contribution requirements are determined each year as part of the actuarial valuation of the plan and may change as a result of plan experience.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p style="margin-top: 0pt; margin-left: 21.6pt; margin-bottom: 9pt;"&gt;The Economic Growth and Tax Relief Act of 2001 (&amp;#8220;EGTRA&amp;#8221;) sets limits on the compensation to be used in the calculation of pension benefits for qualified plans. In order to restore potential lost benefits, the Cooperative has established a Pension Restoration Plan. Under the plan, the amount that NRECA invoices the Cooperative will continue to be based on the full compensation paid to each employee. Upon retirement or quasi retirement of a covered employee, NRECA will calculate the retirement and security benefit to be paid with consideration of the compensation limits and will pay the maximum benefit thereunder. NRECA will also calculate the retirement and security benefit that would have been available without consideration of the compensation limits and the Cooperative will pay the difference, or in the event of quasi retirement, set aside the difference for payment to the employee at retirement. NRECA will then give the Cooperative a credit against future retirement and security contribution liabilities in the amount paid by the Cooperative to the covered employee.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p style="margin-top: 0pt; margin-left: 21.6pt; margin-bottom: 9pt;"&gt;The Cooperative&amp;#8217;s employees with one&amp;#160;year of service who have attained age&amp;#160;21 may participate in a contributory&amp;#160;401(k)&amp;#160;Savings Plan to which the Cooperative will match up to 5% of the effective salary (as defined) of each participating employee. In addition, the Cooperative&amp;#8217;s employees receive an additional 0.5% employer contribution. The Cooperative contributed approximately $1,036,000 and $976,000 to the 401(k) Savings Plan for the years ended December&amp;#160;31, 2025 and 2024, respectively.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p style="margin-top: 0pt; margin-left: 21.6pt; margin-bottom: 9pt;"&gt;The Cooperative established an Executive Deferred Compensation Plan for certain trustees, officers, directors, committee members, and key employees of the Cooperative. This nonqualified plan allows a participant to elect to defer salary into an investment account maintained by the Cooperative. As part of this plan, the Cooperative has recorded the plan investments in other noncurrent assets and a corresponding liability for the plan balance. As of December&amp;#160;31, 2025 and 2024, the notional investments of the plan consisted of marketable securities. As of December&amp;#160;31, 2025 and 2024, the plan&amp;#8217;s balance was approximately $7,070,000 and $6,762,000, respectively, and is included in long&amp;#8209;term accrued liabilities in the accompanying balance sheets.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p style="margin-top: 0pt; margin-left: 21.6pt; margin-bottom: 9pt;"&gt;As participation in the Deferred Compensation Plan will reduce the total salary available in relation to the benefit calculation under the NRECA Retirement and Security Defined Benefit Pension Plan and/or the Pension Restoration Plan, the Cooperative established the Executive Deferred Compensation Pension Restoration Plan. This plan provides retirement benefits to its participants based on years of service and the amount of salary deferred into the Deferred Compensation Plan. The amount of retirement benefits is determined using the same factors used in the Retirement and Security Defined Benefit Pension Plan administered by NRECA and the Pension Restoration Plan, but calculated without regard to limits on compensation for qualified plans under EGTRA, and as if elective deferrals under the Deferred Compensation Plan had been paid in the year of deferral. The Executive Deferred Compensation Pension Restoration Plan had a total funded liability of approximately $556,000 and $513,000 as of December&amp;#160;31, 2025 and 2024, respectively, and is included in long&amp;#8209;term accrued liabilities in the accompanying balance sheets.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p style="margin-top: 0pt; margin-left: 21.6pt; margin-bottom: 9pt;"&gt;Employees of the Cooperative are permitted to sell back to the Cooperative unused personal leave time. The Cooperative has recorded the balance of unused employee personal leave that was earned and available to be sold or used as an accrued liability totaling approximately $3,715,000 and $3,252,000 as of December&amp;#160;31, 2025 and 2024, respectively. In addition, the Cooperative has an unfunded liability of approximately $420,000 and $423,000 as of December&amp;#160;31, 2025 and 2024, respectively, for employees to purchase post&amp;#8209;retirement medical/dental insurance coverage and/or COBRA medical coverage. Both obligations are included in accrued liabilities in the accompanying balance sheets.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p style="margin-top: 0pt; margin-left: 21.6pt; margin-bottom: 9pt; text-indent: -21.6pt; page-break-after: avoid; -aw-import: list-item; -aw-list-level-number: 0; -aw-list-number-format: '%0.'; -aw-list-number-styles: 'decimal'; -aw-list-number-values: '7'; -aw-list-padding-sml: 13.09pt;"&gt;&lt;font style="-aw-import: ignore;"&gt;&lt;font style="font-weight: bold; text-transform: uppercase;"&gt;7.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;font style="width: 13.09pt; font: 7pt 'Times New Roman'; display: inline-block; -aw-import: spaces;"&gt;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160; &lt;/font&gt;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;font style="font-weight: bold; text-transform: uppercase;"&gt;COMMITMENTS AND CONTINGENCIES&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p style="margin-top: 0pt; margin-left: 21.6pt; margin-bottom: 9pt;"&gt;&lt;font style="font-weight: bold;"&gt;Litigation&lt;/font&gt;&amp;#8212;The Cooperative is subject to legal proceedings and claims which arise in the ordinary course of business, some of which are covered by insurance. In the opinion of the Cooperative, the ultimate liability with respect to these proceedings and claims will not have a material adverse effect upon the Cooperative&amp;#8217;s financial position or results of operations.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p style="margin-top: 0pt; margin-left: 21.6pt; margin-bottom: 9pt;"&gt;&lt;font style="font-weight: bold;"&gt;Asset Retirement Obligations&lt;/font&gt;&amp;#8212;A significant portion of the Cooperative&amp;#8217;s transmission facilities are located upon land that is leased from the U.S. Federal and certain state governments. Upon termination of the leases, the structures, improvements, and equipment are to be removed and the land is to be restored. The Cooperative is not able to reasonably estimate the ARO associated with these assets because information sufficient to reasonably estimate the settlement date or range of settlement dates does not exist, and therefore the settlement date of the obligation is indeterminate. Land rights and transmission facilities will be maintained for the foreseeable future; as such, the Cooperative has not recognized a liability related to these obligations. The Cooperative will recognize a liability in the period in which sufficient information is available to reasonably estimate its fair value.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p style="margin-top: 0pt; margin-left: 21.6pt; margin-bottom: 9pt;"&gt;The Bonanza generation facilities are generally located on property owned by the Cooperative. Other than described below, it is the Cooperative&amp;#8217;s opinion that the Cooperative does not have a reclamation liability related to the Bonanza generation facilities.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p style="margin-top: 0pt; margin-left: 21.6pt; margin-bottom: 9pt;"&gt;In 2015, the EPA published the Disposal of Coal Combustion Residual (&amp;#8220;CCR&amp;#8221;) from Electric Utilities final rule (&amp;#8220;CCR Rule&amp;#8221;) in the Federal Register. The CCR Rule regulates the disposal of CCR, including coal ash and gypsum, as non&amp;#8209;hazardous solid waste in CCR units at active generating power plants. As of December&amp;#160;31, 2025 and 2024, the ARO related to the CCR Rule totaled approximately $5,805,000 and $4,214,000, respectively. During the year ended December 31, 2025, the Cooperative recorded an increase to the ARO of approximately $1,381,000 due to a change in the estimated timing of the settlement and cost of the ARO. During the years ended December&amp;#160;31, 2025 and 2024, accretion expense totaled approximately $210,000 and $200,000, respectively. The Cooperative will update its estimation of the ARO as additional information becomes available.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p style="margin-top: 0pt; margin-left: 21.6pt; margin-bottom: 9pt; text-indent: -21.6pt; page-break-after: avoid; -aw-import: list-item; -aw-list-level-number: 0; -aw-list-number-format: '%0.'; -aw-list-number-styles: 'decimal'; -aw-list-number-values: '8'; -aw-list-padding-sml: 13.09pt;"&gt;&lt;font style="-aw-import: ignore;"&gt;&lt;font style="font-weight: bold; text-transform: uppercase;"&gt;8.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;font style="width: 13.09pt; font: 7pt 'Times New Roman'; display: inline-block; -aw-import: spaces;"&gt;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160;&amp;#160; &lt;/font&gt;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;font style="font-weight: bold; text-transform: uppercase;"&gt;DISCLOSURES ABOUT FAIR VALUE OF FINANCIAL INSTRUMENTS&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p style="margin-top: 0pt; margin-left: 21.6pt; margin-bottom: 9pt; page-break-after: avoid;"&gt;Accounting principles establish a fair value hierarchy, which gives precedence to fair value measurements calculated using observable inputs to those using unobservable inputs. The standard also requires entities to disclose the fair value of financial instruments according to the following established hierarchy:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p style="margin-top: 0pt; margin-left: 21.6pt; margin-bottom: 9pt;"&gt;&lt;font style="font-weight: bold; font-style: italic;"&gt;Level&lt;/font&gt;&lt;font style="font-weight: bold; font-style: italic;"&gt; &amp;#160;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;font style="font-weight: bold; font-style: italic;"&gt;1&lt;/font&gt;&amp;#8212;Inputs that are quoted prices (unadjusted) in active markets for identical assets or liabilities that the reporting entity has the ability to access at the measurement date.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p style="margin-top: 0pt; margin-left: 21.6pt; margin-bottom: 9pt;"&gt;&lt;font style="font-weight: bold; font-style: italic;"&gt;Level&lt;/font&gt;&lt;font style="font-weight: bold; font-style: italic;"&gt; &amp;#160;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;font style="font-weight: bold; font-style: italic;"&gt;2&lt;/font&gt;&amp;#8212;Inputs other than quoted prices included in Level&amp;#160;1 that are observable for the asset or liability, either directly or indirectly.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p style="margin-top: 0pt; margin-left: 21.6pt; margin-bottom: 9pt;"&gt;&lt;font style="font-weight: bold; font-style: italic;"&gt;Level&lt;/font&gt;&lt;font style="font-weight: bold; font-style: italic;"&gt; &amp;#160;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;font style="font-weight: bold; font-style: italic;"&gt;3&lt;/font&gt;&amp;#8212;Unobservable inputs for the asset or liability. These are inputs for which there is no market data available, or observable inputs that are adjusted using Level&amp;#160;3 assumptions.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p style="margin-top: 0pt; margin-left: 21.6pt; margin-bottom: 9pt; page-break-after: avoid;"&gt;The following table sets forth the fair value hierarchy by level of the Cooperative&amp;#8217;s recurring fair value financial instruments.&lt;/p&gt;
